8. SEMANTIC INTERPRETATION

Build a general interpretation stage that converts the question into explicit engineering concepts.

Distinguish:

- Explicitly stated facts.
- Mathematically implied facts.
- Requested quantities.
- Unknowns.
- Uncertainties.
- Assumptions.
- Quantities that cannot be inferred from available evidence.

The same geometric condition may appear through different vocabulary, sentence structures, abbreviations, or indirect descriptions.

Normalize equivalent meanings into a consistent internal representation.

Never treat different geometric concepts as equivalent merely because their wording is similar.

PRESERVE THESE DISTINCTIONS

- True-line inclination versus front-view angle.
- True-line inclination versus top-view angle.
- Angle to HP versus angle to VP.
- Angle with XY/XV versus angle with a projection plane.
- True length versus front-view length.
- True length versus top-view length.
- Point height above HP versus point depth in front of VP.
- Line orientation versus endpoint location.
- Given measurement versus requested measurement.
- Explicit value versus derived value.

Interpret each angle using the object it describes, its reference, and the projection or plane involved.

Example:

If the question says "the front view makes an angle of 40 degrees with XY", do not automatically convert it into "inclination to HP = 40 degrees".

If the question says "the line is inclined at 40 degrees to HP", do not automatically interpret it as a front-view angle.

If wording is genuinely ambiguous, preserve the possible interpretations and request clarification when they produce different solutions.

